    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d73">

                <pb facs="0223" n="197" xml:id="pg197"/>

                <head>73. Telegram From the Mission at the United Nations to the Department of
                        State<anchor n="1" xml:id="d73-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#italic"><placeName>New
                                York</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                                when="1957-06-12T18:00:00-04:00">June 12, 1957—6
                        p.m.</date></hi></dateline></opener>
                <p>1065. From Lodge. Reappointment of SYGUN—Deptel 935, mytel 1055.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d73-f2"/></p>
                <p>This morning I informed Hammarskjold of our hope he would be willing accept
                    reappointment, and that I intended consult with British, French and Chinese.
                    Before doing so, however, I had wanted inform him.</p>
                <p>Hammarskjold said he was no more a candidate for reelection now than he was
                    candidate for election in 1953. He was willing, as he put it, to be “ordered” to
                    continue or not as members desired. He would be happy either way. He went on to
                    say there should be no ambiguity about position of SYG if he was to be
                    effective, nor could anyone who “sought” post be effective in his opinion. The
                    SYG had to have the strongest backing and therefore it could not be matter of
                    personal ambition. Hammarskjold said he greatly appreciated confidence we had
                    shown in him.</p>
                <p>He was quite agreeable to my consulting with the others which I am arranging to
                    do early next week.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Wadsworth</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
